          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="alternatives" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">10. Alternatives Outside Bowflex</h2>
<p>
            The T9's figures put it against several well-known machines, and the right comparison depends on
            what you value.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you want a big screen and classes, not an open console:</strong> the
            <a href="/nordictrack-commercial-1750-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">NordicTrack Commercial 1750</a>
            has a 22 by 60-inch deck, decline to -3%, a 16-inch pivoting touchscreen and lifetime motor cover,
            at a higher price and built around a paid iFIT membership. Our
            <a href="/best-smart-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best smart treadmills</a>
            roundup sets out the trade-off between a screen-led machine and an open one like the T9.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you will never pay for an app:</strong> the T9 already suits you; our guide to
            <a href="/best-treadmills-without-subscriptions/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmills without subscriptions</a>
            covers others that work fully on their own.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you need decline:</strong> the T9 has none, so look at the Commercial 1750 or the
            machines in our <a href="/best-treadmills-with-decline/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">decline roundup</a>.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you mainly walk:</strong> a walking-focused machine does the job for less; our
            <a href="/best-walking-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">walking treadmill roundup</a>
            covers the options.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">11. Who Should Buy the Bowflex T9</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Buy it if you run and want room on the belt.</strong> The 22-inch deck is the T9's defining
            feature, and for tall or broad runners it is the right reason to choose it over the T6 or a typical
            20-inch folding treadmill.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Buy it if you are a heavier user.</strong> A 350 lb rating with a lifetime frame and motor
            warranty gives more margin than the T6's 325 lb, and the motor cover is reassurance for long, steep
            sessions.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Buy it if you want incline without a subscription.</strong> Fifteen percent of motorised
            lift and a console that works on its own, with the option to connect JRNY, Peloton or Zwift, is an
            unusual combination at this price.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Think twice if heart-rate training through an app is central to you</strong>, because of the
            Bluetooth limitation both reviews report, and if you want a soft deck, because both describe it as
            firm.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Choose the T6 if you mainly walk</strong> or need it to fold smaller, and the T16 if a
            built-in touchscreen is what will keep you on the machine.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Buy it on sale.</strong> It was $1,399 against $1,799 regular when we checked, and Bowflex
            discounts often. Compare the delivered price, including the delivery option you need, before you
            order.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">12. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What do Bowflex T9 treadmill reviews say?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Independent reviews agree it is a sturdy, quiet folding treadmill with a 22 by 60-inch deck, a generous 15% incline and easy dial controls. The two consistent complaints are that Bluetooth cannot connect a heart-rate strap and an app at the same time, and that the cushioning is firmer than some prefer. We have not tested it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is the Bowflex T9 a good treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                On its published figures, yes, particularly for runners and heavier users: 22 by 60 inches, 0 to 15% incline, 12 mph, a 350 lb rating, and lifetime frame and motor cover with three years on parts. Its 3.5 HP is a test-setting rating rather than a continuous-duty figure, and labour cover is one year.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Bowflex T9 vs T6: which should I buy?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Both have 15% incline, 12 mph and lifetime frame and motor cover. The T9 adds a 22-inch-wide deck instead of 20, a 350 lb rating instead of 325, a higher motor rating on Bowflex’s test basis and a colour console. Tall or broad runners and heavier users benefit most; walkers rarely need it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does the Bowflex T9 need a JRNY subscription?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. The T9 works fully without any subscription. It connects over Bluetooth to JRNY, Peloton and Zwift if you want app content, but each is a separate paid subscription, so check the current price of the one you want.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does the Bowflex T9 fold?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, but not flat. Bowflex lists it at 77.2 inches long assembled and 48.6 inches long folded, staying 37 inches wide and 67.6 inches tall. It weighs 282 lb and rolls on two transport wheels. Check ceiling height too: Bowflex recommends the user’s height plus 20.75 inches.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the weight limit on the Bowflex T9?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Bowflex lists a maximum user weight of 350 lb, and independent reviews give the same figure. That is 25 lb more than the T6.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes Buying a Bowflex T9</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">The T9 is a straightforward machine with a few figures that are easy to misread. These are the errors that cost buyers money, space or patience.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing 3.5 HP with another brand’s CHP</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Bowflex says its 3.5 HP is a test-setting rating that does not reflect operational horsepower. Setting it against a competitor’s 3.5 CHP and calling them equal, or calling either stronger, compares two different measurements. Use the lifetime motor warranty as the confidence signal instead.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Planning app-based heart-rate training without checking the Bluetooth</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Both independent reviews we read report that the T9 cannot connect a heart-rate strap and an app at the same time. If that is how you train, pair the strap to the app instead, and test your setup early in the 30-day return window.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Forgetting the ceiling</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Bowflex’s guidance is the user’s height plus 20.75 inches, which puts a 6 ft 5 in runner above a standard 8-foot ceiling. Tall runners are the T9’s best buyers and the ones this affects most, so measure the lowest point above the belt before ordering.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Paying the regular price</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">The T9 was $1,799 regular and $1,399 on sale on the day we checked, and Bowflex discounts often. Watch the price, and compare the delivered total, including the delivery option you need, before you buy.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

